00:37:10.515 --> 00:37:14.835
Mr. Hanfield also started looking at our report and then, and, or looking at our,

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:17.280
you know, application and then

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.080
advised, recommended that we start talking to the,

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:22.640
Army Corps of Engineers,

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:26.400
which of course we did. It was a little bit hard to get in touch with them, but eventually

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:34.205
we did get, we did connect with somebody there and indeed they informed us that this crossing was going to be in their jurisdiction.

00:37:36.205 --> 00:37:39.965
And we are going to, you know, we're in the process of doing, getting

00:37:39.965 --> 00:37:41.085
a general permit from them.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.450
We've also modified

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:45.250
the plans.

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:51.650
Their requirements for stream crossings, even though this is an intermittent stream, they consider this a stream and they have,

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:52.770
standards

00:37:54.105 --> 00:37:58.665
that we're, you know, that you should meet when you're, when you're crossing something in their jurisdiction.

00:38:00.185 --> 00:38:03.385
So we modified the, modified the plans to meet their requirements.

00:38:04.025 --> 00:38:09.305
And then of course, we've also added in the so called icing on the cake. We've added the mitigation

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.680
elsewhere on the property. We're going to remove invasive species,

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:14.760
plant species and plant,

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:20.280
you know, something better to be growing in a wetlands area.

00:38:22.685 --> 00:38:29.085
Now there's still work that Mr. Bushnell is going to do to work with Mr. Hanfield. So I'm not going to talk about the,

00:38:33.325 --> 00:38:44.430
you know, the flow dynamics, so the capacity of the system and the potential upstream impact. So I do want Mr. Allard to know that, you know, we've heard him, we take what he's saying very seriously,

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:49.310
and we would, you know, we're being very careful about the design,

00:38:49.550 --> 00:38:50.430
for that reason.

00:38:54.685 --> 00:38:56.285
Want, like Mr.

00:38:56.445 --> 00:38:59.085
Gruner, and since Commissioner

00:38:59.085 --> 00:39:00.125
Hallisey mentioned,

00:39:00.605 --> 00:39:02.525
his report and his opinion,

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:06.170
If you read Mr. Gruner's report, he had two,

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:12.970
he obviously, his primary recommendation was a bridge really elevating above the channel totally,

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:15.050
to minimize the impact.

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:17.290
But a secondary

00:39:17.875 --> 00:39:19.555
recommendation that he made

00:39:19.955 --> 00:39:22.995
was not dissimilar to what we have now.

00:39:23.395 --> 00:39:24.435
He recommended

00:39:24.755 --> 00:39:28.435
one open box or, open bottom culvert

00:39:28.675 --> 00:39:31.315
and he recommended placing it on the Eastern Channel,

00:39:31.555 --> 00:39:33.075
the Eastern stream bed.

00:39:33.315 --> 00:39:37.630
And the reason for that most likely was because it's wider and he's concerned,

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:40.350
about channelization of the water.

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:48.750
What our design is, is one box culvert, which is a little bit different than an open bottom in that,

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:54.005
we we've got to restore the stream bed afterwards, but it is part of our design to put,

00:39:54.805 --> 00:39:55.605
the

00:39:55.765 --> 00:39:56.485
stream

00:39:56.565 --> 00:39:59.845
material back into it. It will function like an open bottom culvert.

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:06.260
And now it's on the Western Channel, not the Eastern Channel. And the reason for that is our,

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:09.620
consulting ecologists and engineer agreed

00:40:09.620 --> 00:40:11.380
that there's a larger watershed

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:12.500
on that side

00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:14.500
and that if

00:40:15.895 --> 00:40:20.615
animals were to, you know, small, mid sized wildlife were to approach the crossing,

00:40:20.935 --> 00:40:24.775
they were more likely to prefer a flatter gradient. And that's where that,

00:40:25.175 --> 00:40:31.530
it's steeper on the other side, on the Eastern Channel. So for those two reasons, it did make sense to put box

00:40:31.610 --> 00:40:35.130
on that side. Now it also happens that that's the side right up against

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:36.250
the

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:38.250
properties on Pine Ridge Drive.

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:39.290
So

00:40:40.090 --> 00:40:44.330
if there is some risk of obstruction or other things that would cause backup,

00:40:46.545 --> 00:40:49.505
they've been minimized by us moving it and putting it on that side.

00:40:50.785 --> 00:40:51.425
Now,

00:40:53.505 --> 00:40:55.025
just to address that last,

00:40:55.585 --> 00:40:57.025
the channelization,

00:40:57.025 --> 00:40:58.225
the thing that Mr. Gruner,

00:40:58.630 --> 00:41:00.950
you know, has brought up, revisited a few times.

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:08.630
I mean, would just ask the commission to go back to, the first report submitted by our, consultant, Mr. Logan.

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:20.235
There's photos. If you don't recall them, revisit the report and look at them. Immediately south of the site of the crossing, the water, the water, and the stream beds like converge under, under really wet conditions.

00:41:22.555 --> 00:41:23.675
The important

00:41:23.675 --> 00:41:28.590
part of our design, think on the side where there's still a three foot round pipe,

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:31.310
which by the way will be embedded,

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:38.110
is at the part where the water, the part where the water exits is going to have riprap,

00:41:38.110 --> 00:41:39.470
right? And it's the,

00:41:40.205 --> 00:41:43.085
at the, the direct purpose of the riprap

00:41:43.085 --> 00:41:59.610
is to dissipate the energy of the water flowing out of it, slow the velocity, and spread it back out over the area. If you look, if you look at the plans, you can see that it's wider than, it's far wider than where the water just exits. It's really got the purpose of spreading it back out to an area similar to what,

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:02.170
the footprint of the bed was to begin with.

00:42:04.810 --> 00:42:06.570
And the recurring,

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:10.890
you know, the recurring discussion about the bridge, I just want to revisit it one more time

00:42:11.355 --> 00:42:12.075
and,

00:42:14.635 --> 00:42:15.435
you know,

00:42:16.635 --> 00:42:19.355
